---
// Lesson 10.1b — 2-Button Jam: Build Day 1
// rpg jam day: core loop first — the two verbs working end to end.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>10.1b — 2-Button Jam: Build Day 1</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Build Day 1 rule: end the day with the core loop — BOTH verbs
    working, something to act on, a lose state. Ugly is fine;
    non-playable is not. A jam that leaves Day 1 without its loop
    spends Day 3 building the loop instead of the game.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Day</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~50 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Build order (the loop skeleton):</strong></p>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Read both inputs</strong> — one line each,
            GetKeyDown. Print to console on press. Nothing else
            works until this does:</li>
    </ol>
    <pre><code>if (Input.GetKeyDown(KeyCode.LeftArrow)) Debug.Log("A");
if (Input.GetKeyDown(KeyCode.RightArrow)) Debug.Log("B");</code></pre>
    <ol start="2">
        <li><strong>Verb 1 with a placeholder</strong> — say DUCK:
            scale the player sprite to 0.5 height. One line of
            code, instantly testable, already FUN to spam.</li>
        <li><strong>Verb 2 with a placeholder</strong> — DASH: a
            0.15s Translate burst in facing direction. Your
            <a href="3.3a.html">3.3a</a> cooldown pattern IS the
            dash (it is a fire rate on a movement).</li>
        <li><strong>Something to act on</strong> — one obstacle, one
            pickup, one enemy. ANY one. Borrow the starter's spike
            or coin prefab wholesale.</li>
        <li><strong>One lose state</strong> — the death loop you
            have written twice (<a href="2.2a.html">2.2a</a>);
            here it can just be "restart the scene."</li>
    </ol>

    <p><strong>Checkpoint ritual (last 10 minutes):</strong> pass
    the keyboard around the team — each member plays the loop for
    30 seconds. Anyone who cannot play it cannot build on it.</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Jam-day smells</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>You wrote 80 lines and the verbs do not BOTH work.
                Which will you cut first — and why does the
                console-print ritual (step 1) catch input bugs
                before they hide inside mechanics?</li>
            <li>The dash works but feels bad (no cooldown mercy,
                no <a href="6.3a.html">6.3a</a> buffer). When do
                you tune feel — now, or after the loop exists?
                Why?</li>
            <li>One team member has written nothing while two
                built. Split by VERB tomorrow — why does
                "one person owns the whole game" kill jams
                (merge conflicts AND bus factor)?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Close of Day</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~5 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Loop proof</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Record the loop (even phone video) — 10 seconds, all
                verbs. Commit + push: <code>"10.1b: loop skeleton"</code>.</li>
            <li>Write tomorrow's ONE riskiest task on a sticky note
                (the thing that could kill the game — build it
                FIRST tomorrow, <a href="10.1c.html">10.1c</a>).</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Play your own loop: both verbs, one obstacle, one lose state</li>
            <li>Explain the build order and why inputs print before mechanics</li>
            <li>Name your riskiest task for Day 2</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-10/index.html">Unit 10: Jam Season</a> — jam build orders</li>
        <li><a href="10.1a.html">10.1a — Kickoff</a> — your pitch card</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="10.1c.html">10.1c — 2-Button Jam: Build Day 2</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="10.1b" title="10.1b 2-Button Jam: Build Day 1 — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
